If you need to capture both sign-in and sign-up users from Azure AD B2C and insert them into your on-prem SQL Server, you can achieve this by enabling and routing the appropriate diagnostic logs to Event Grid. This way, you track:

1. Sign-ups – Logged in AuditLogs and ProvisioningLogs as UserCreated events.


2. Sign-ins – Logged in SignInLogs.




---

How to Capture Both Sign-In and Sign-Up Events:

1. Enable Diagnostic Logs for All Events:

1. Go to Azure Portal → Azure AD B2C.


2. Select Audit Logs → Diagnostic Settings.


3. Click + Add Diagnostic Setting.


4. Under Category Details, select:

✅ AuditLogs – (Tracks user creation, updates, and deletions).

✅ SignInLogs – (Tracks user sign-ins).

✅ ProvisioningLogs – (Captures social/external provider sign-ups).



5. Under Destination Details, choose:

Send to Event Grid → Yes



6. Click Save.




---

2. Create Event Grid Subscription for Both Sign-In and Sign-Up:

1. Go to Event Grid → Event Subscriptions.


2. Click + Create Event Subscription.


3. Configure the following:

Topic Type: Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD B2C)

Source Resource: Select your Azure AD B2C tenant.

Event Types:

✅ Microsoft Entra ID > User Created (Sign-up)

✅ Microsoft Entra ID > User Signed In (Sign-in)


Endpoint Type: Azure Function or Logic App.

Function URL: Select your function that inserts user data into SQL Server.



4. Click Create.




---

3. Azure Function to Insert Users into SQL Server:

The Azure Function will receive both sign-up and sign-in events and insert/update user records in SQL Server.

C# Example – Insert/Update Users on Sign-Up or Sign-In:

using System;
using System.Data.SqlClient;
using Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public static class B2CEventTrigger
{
    [Function("B2CEventTrigger")]
    public static void Run(
        [EventGridTrigger] EventGridEvent eventGridEvent,
        FunctionContext context)
    {
        var logger = context.GetLogger("B2CEventTrigger");
        logger.LogInformation($"Event Received: {eventGridEvent.EventType}");

        var userData = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<UserData>(eventGridEvent.Data.ToString());

        string connectionString = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("SQL_CONNECTION_STRING");

        using (SqlConnection conn = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
        {
            conn.Open();
            var query = @"
                IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Users WHERE Email = @Email)
                UPDATE Users SET LastSignIn = GETDATE()
                WHERE Email = @Email
                ELSE
                INSERT INTO Users (UserName, Email, SignUpDate, LastSignIn)
                VALUES (@UserName, @Email, GETDATE(), GETDATE())";

            using (SqlCommand cmd = new SqlCommand(query, conn))
            {
                cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue("@UserName", userData.UserName ?? "Unknown");
                cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue("@Email", userData.Email);
                cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();
            }
        }
    }
}

public class UserData
{
    public string UserName { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; }
}


---

4. Handling Duplicate Users (Sign-Up vs. Sign-In):

On Sign-Up: Insert a new record in SQL.

On Sign-In:

If the user already exists, update the LastSignIn timestamp.

If the user doesn’t exist (rare case), insert them as a new user.




---

5. Testing and Monitoring:

1. Perform a new user sign-up through your Azure AD B2C user flow.


2. Perform a sign-in as the same user.


3. Check SQL Server to ensure the record exists and LastSignIn is updated.




---

Advantages of Capturing Both Events:

Complete User Tracking: Monitor user lifecycle from sign-up to ongoing sign-ins.

Engagement Metrics: Track active users and sign-in frequency.

Security and Auditing: Log sign-ins for compliance and detect unusual activity.



